  "cut_point": "We are given a grid rectangle with sides $ a $ and $ b $, where $ a $ and $ b $ are natural numbers. The rectangle is **not** possible to cut out a sub-rectangle (along the grid lines) that has an area **almost equal** to half of the original rectangle.\n\nWe define **\"almost equal\"** as two numbers being either equal or differing by at most one unit.\n\nSo, if the original area is $ A = ab $, then **half the area** is $ \\frac{ab}{2} $. We want to find the **smallest possible** value of $ |a - b| $ such that **no** sub-rectangle (with integer side lengths) has an area that is **almost equal** to $ \\frac{ab}{2} $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the condition\n\nWe want to **avoid** any sub-rectangle with area $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{ab}{2} \\right\\rfloor $, $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{ab}{2} \\right\\rfloor + 1 $, or $ \\left\\lceil \\frac{ab}{2} \\right\\rceil $, since those are the areas that are \"almost equal\" to $ \\frac{ab}{2} $.",
